%decision ContentAddressedIdentityIsBytesNotLabels
  what:    A file's identity is (crc32, size_bytes); the CRC32 is also embedded in folder/file names (~XXXXXXXX) and re-derived from the on-disk bytes at every stage. Names are derived data, never the source of truth.
  why:     The whole point of the project: the same title ripped twice is two distinct rows, a renamed or relocated folder is still the same row. Identity survives every rename because it is a hash of the video bytes, not a path. files has UNIQUE (crc32, size_bytes); ON CONFLICT upserts. That pair is the primary identity everywhere downstream (associations.file_id, probe_cache, move verification). Folder-embedded CRC (parseFolderName reading ~XXXXXXXX) is a CONVENIENCE for fast skip and human inspection, NOT trusted: processOne re-probes and corrects a folder whose claimed CRC disagrees with the bytes. Consequence: every CRC comparison in the pipeline is uppercase (runRhashCrc32 uppercases+trims); any code that introduces a lowercase or untrimmed CRC will silently fail to match.
  affects: Stage.runRhashCrc32, Stage.upsertFileSql, Move.verifyFile, uniDork.processOne, Nfo.parseFolderName

%decision StageAndMediaKindPartitionOneFilesTable
  what:    One files table is partitioned by two columns: stage (staging -> buffer -> library) and media_kind (movie | episode). The resolvers select on these, they are not separate tables.
  why:     Movies and TV share the same files table, the same identity model, and the same three-directory discipline; only the two partition columns separate them. resolve matches media_kind='movie' AND stage<>'buffer' (selectUnresolvedSql); tv-resolve matches media_kind='episode' AND stage='staging' (selectUnresolvedTvSql). stage='buffer' is deliberately EXCLUDED from movie resolve: once rename has written a folder into the buffer, its identity comes from the NFO rename wrote, not from a fresh TMDB match. Re-resolving a buffered file would fight the rename that already happened. rename selects stage='staging' (selectRenamableSql / selectEpisodeRenamableSql), move flips rows to stage='library'. The lifecycle is encoded in this column, not in code flow.
  affects: Resolve.selectUnresolvedSql, Tv.Resolve.selectUnresolvedTvSql, Rename.selectRenamableSql, Tv.Rename.selectEpisodeRenamableSql

%decision MoveBlastRadiusIsOneFolderUnderRoot
  what:    move/tv-move only ever WRITE under the configured library root and only ever DELETE from the buffer; every destination is guarded by enforceUnderRoot before any filesystem mutation.
  why:     This is the single most dangerous verb in the system: it relocates real media and deletes the source. The safety property is mechanical, not careful coding: enforceUnderRoot rejects any destination not prefixed by libraryRoot + '/'. move additionally aborts if the destination folder already exists, so it can never overwrite an existing library folder. The blast radius of one movie move is exactly: one folder added to library, one removed from buffer. tv-move is a deliberately WIDER blast radius (it merges into an existing show folder via mergeTree), but still never overwrites an existing episode file and still only writes under tvLibrary. The library root comes from UNIDORK_PATH_LIBRARY / UNIDORK_PATH_TV_LIBRARY (set from nix/config.nix). Those env vars are the ONLY thing controlling where move writes; this is why the README tells you to echo them before the first production move.
  affects: Move.moveFolder, Move.transferFolder, Move.enforceUnderRoot, Tv.Move.moveShow, Tv.Move.transferShow

%decision IdentityVerifiedBySizeNotByRehash
  what:    At promotion, a file's stored CRC is trusted as long as the on-disk size equals the stored size_bytes. There is no re-hash at move time; on size mismatch (or missing row) the update falls back to a name-based path update.
  why:     Re-running rhash on every file at promotion would double the I/O cost of the slowest part of the pipeline for files that have not changed since probe. Size equality is a cheap proxy: a file whose byte count is unchanged has overwhelmingly not had its CRC change. verifyFile / verifyEpisode return (path, diskOk, identOpt) where diskOk is purely the size comparison. updateMovieFile / updateEpisode then branch: verified -> update files row by (crc, size); unverified -> update by old path string, and print a WARN. The honest hole: a file corrupted or swapped in place at exactly the same byte length would pass verification with a stale CRC. This is accepted as vanishingly unlikely against the I/O savings. See open issue NoRematchGateAtPromotion for the related trust of the NFO id.
  affects: Move.verifyFile, Move.updateMovieFile, Tv.Move.verifyEpisode, Tv.Move.updateEpisode

%decision AtomicRenameOrRsyncPartialThenCommit
  what:    Same-filesystem transfers use an atomic mv -T; cross-filesystem transfers rsync into a .unidork-partial staging dir under the library root, then mv-commit into place, then delete the source. Crashed partials are swept on the next run.
  why:     A cross-device move is not atomic: a crash mid-copy would leave a half-written folder in the live library. Staging the copy under .unidork-partial and only mv-committing the completed copy makes the visible-in-library step atomic even across filesystems. sameDevice compares stat %d (device numbers); a stat FAILURE returns false, which routes to the SAFER rsync-partial path rather than risking an atomic rename that would actually be a cross-device copy. sweepPartial removes the whole .unidork-partial tree at the start of a non-dry-run move, on the theory that any partial present is the debris of a previous crash, never a concurrent transfer (the pipeline is single-writer). On a cross-device abort the SOURCE is left intact (delete only happens after a successful commit), so a failed move never loses data.
  affects: Move.transferFolder, Move.sweepPartial, Move.partialRoot, Tv.Move.transferShow

%decision RuntimeNormalizedClusteringForEditions
  what:    Versions clusters a tmdb_id's copies into editions by normalizing each copy's runtime to a reference frame rate (24000/1001) and grouping copies whose normalized seconds are within 1.5% of each other.
  why:     The naive approach (group by raw duration) would split a PAL 25fps transfer and a 23.976fps transfer of the SAME cut into two editions because PAL speedup shortens the runtime by ~4%. normalizedSeconds multiplies durationSec by fps/referenceFps to cancel the speedup. relativeClose uses a RELATIVE tolerance (d/hi <= sameCutTolerance = 1.5e-2), not an absolute second count, so the tolerance scales with film length: a 3-hour film tolerates more absolute drift than a 90-minute one. The 1.5% threshold is tuned to merge frame-rate and minor-trim differences while still separating a genuine Director's/Extended cut (which differ by minutes, well outside 1.5%). See open issue DuplicatesNotRankedForUser for what clustering does NOT yet do.
  affects: Versions.normalizedSeconds, Versions.clusterByCut, Versions.relativeClose, Versions.referenceFps

%decision DatabaseAndMetadataAreAbilitiesInterpretedAtRunBoundary
  what:    Per-file logic (resolveOne, runStep, etc.) is written against abilities (MovieCatalog/ResolveCatalog/MetaApi/TvCatalog/TvMetaApi/RenameCatalog/TvMoveCatalog). The real Postgres/HTTP handlers are installed only at the runWith / run boundary; the per-file functions never touch a connection directly.
  why:     This is what makes resolveOne and friends unit-testable with scripted pure handlers. The Resolve.resolveOne.* and Tv.Resolve.resolveOne.* tests install handlers that return canned TMDB responses and RECORD whether recordMatch was called, with zero database or network. The effect rows in the signature (e.g. {MetaApi, ResolveCatalog}) are the contract: any handler satisfying them can drive the logic. The production handler (metaGo/catalogGo inside runWith) wires the ability to prepared statements; the test handler wires it to a tuple accumulator. Consequence: reading a resolveOne body tells you the decision logic but NOT what hits the DB. The actual writes live in the handler arms of runWith, and the dry-run gate (see DryRunGatesWritesNotMetadata) lives in resolveOne but is enforced by simply not calling the ability.
  affects: Resolve.resolveOne, Resolve.runWith, Tv.Resolve.resolveOne, Tv.Resolve.runWith, Rename.runStep

%decision DryRunGatesFilesystemAndAssociationsNotProbeOrCache
  what:    --dry-run suppresses filesystem operations and association/match writes, but probe rows, TMDB caches, and subtitle-search caches are STILL written during a dry run.
  why:     A dry run is meant to preview file movement and matching decisions safely, not to be a pure read. Probing and caching are idempotent, side-effect-free-on-the-library work whose results are valuable to keep even from a preview run. resolveOne / Tv.Resolve.resolveOne gate recordMatch/recordEpisodeMatch on dryRun (the 'if dryRun then () else record...' pattern), but the surrounding runWith still fetches and caches TMDB details/search/season payloads regardless. move/rename gate the actual mv/rsync/removeFile and the files-row promotion on dryRun, emitting WouldRename/WouldRemoveDuplicate outcomes instead. Consequence: a dry run is NOT free of DB writes. It will populate probe_cache, tmdb_search_cache, tmdb_details_cache, etc. This is intentional but surprising; do not treat --dry-run as read-only.
  affects: Move.run, Rename.runNew, Resolve.resolveOne, Tv.Resolve.resolveOne, Tv.Rename.applyTv

%open_issue RelangDecisionComputedButNeverApplied
  what:     Relang.decide produces a default-audio-track decision (SwitchTo / AlreadyCorrect / OriginalNotPresent / ...), but nothing in the pipeline remuxes or re-flags the file to apply it.
  why:      decide -> selectTrack -> bestOf is a complete, tested analysis: it picks the original-language, non-commentary, highest-channel/best-codec track and reports whether the current default is wrong. The result is a Decision value that is computed and then dropped. There is no ffmpeg remux step and no mkvpropedit call anywhere that consumes a SwitchTo. The README lists this under 'What's next' as the first item. Wiring it requires deciding WHEN to apply (at rename into buffer? at promotion?) and a mux/flag step that mutates the container, which crosses the line from metadata-only into byte-mutating territory and needs its own safety story.
  blocking: actually correcting default audio tracks for foreign-language media
  affects:  Relang.decide, Relang.selectTrack, Relang.bestOf

%open_issue NoSubtitleMuxingAndNoTvSubtitleFetchAtAll
  what:     Movie subtitles are fetched from subdl and written as language-tagged SIDECAR files only; they are never muxed into the MKV, and the TV pipeline fetches no subtitles whatsoever.
  why:      fetchAndSaveByImdb/saveOneLanguage download and write '<base>.<lang>.<ext>' next to the video. maybeFetchSubsM is called only from the MOVIE rename path (executeOne); the TV rename path (executeEpisode) has no equivalent call. Muxing into the container would require an ffmpeg remux step that does not exist (same missing capability as the Relang apply). Consequence: Kodi/Jellyfin will pick up the sidecars for movies, but episodic TV gets no subtitles at all, and no media gets embedded subs. README documents both gaps honestly under 'What's next'.
  blocking: embedded subtitle tracks, any TV subtitle support
  affects:  Subs.fetchAndSaveByImdb, Subs.saveOneLanguage, Rename.maybeFetchSubsM

%open_issue DuplicatesGroupedIntoEditionsButNotRankedForUserDecision
  what:     Exact byte-duplicates (same CRC tag + matching size at the target) are removed at rename, and distinct rips of one tmdb_id are clustered into editions by runtime, but multiple DISTINCT rips of the same cut are never ranked by quality and surfaced for a keep/discard decision.
  why:      executeOne removes a source that is byte-identical to something already at the target (DuplicateRemoved). Versions.analyze clusters by cut and even computes qualityScore and picks a per-edition default. But that is as far as it goes. qualityScore exists (resolution/depth/bitrate/codec/channels weighting) and analyze marks an is_default member, yet nothing presents 'you have a 1080p x264 and a 2160p x265 of the same cut, keep which?' to the user or auto-prunes the loser. The editions/versions tables capture the grouping; the missing piece is a surfacing/pruning verb. README lists this under 'What's next' as duplicate management.
  blocking: automated or assisted pruning of redundant quality variants
  affects:  Versions.analyze, Versions.qualityScore, Rename.executeOne

%open_issue NoRematchGateBeforePromotion
  what:     move trusts the tmdb id written into the buffer NFO by rename and does not re-verify the match against TMDB before promoting into the library.
  why:      moveFolder parses the buffer NFO, reads its tmdb id, and promotes. If rename associated the wrong title, move faithfully promotes the wrong title; the only verification at this stage is byte-identity (size), not correctness of the MATCH. This is by design for now: the buffer is the human review step, and re-matching at promotion would re-incur TMDB calls and could disagree with a deliberate manual correction in the buffer. README flags an optional re-match gate under 'What's next'. If added, it would re-run the resolver against the buffer NFO id and refuse/flag on disagreement.
  blocking: catching a mis-association before it reaches the library
  affects:  Move.moveFolder, Move.updateMovieFile

%open_issue BatchTvResolveNeverPromptsAndAutoRefusesAmbiguousShows
  what:     The TvInteract ability and batchInteract handler exist, but Tv.Resolve.resolveOne never calls chooseShow; ambiguous shows are resolved purely by pickBestShow's popularity tiebreak, and a no-confident-match becomes a silent NoShowMatch refusal.
  why:      batchInteract is a handler that auto-answers chooseShow with None, i.e. 'never pick interactively'. The interactive picker is plumbed as an ability but resolveShowPick decides via scoreShow + pickBestShow and surfaces NoShowMatch when nothing scores high enough. So in batch mode an ambiguous show is refused rather than queued for a human, and there is no --interactive path wired into the TV resolver run (unlike the movie/interactive show selection mentioned for the broader project). Wiring an interactive handler would mean swapping batchInteract for one that prompts, and threading a flag through Tv.Resolve.run / runWith.
  blocking: human disambiguation of ambiguous TV show matches in batch runs
  affects:  Tv.Resolve.resolveOne, Tv.Resolve.batchInteract, Tv.Resolve.resolveShowPick

